{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_persistence_fsm_overview_fsm_pattern", "label": "FSM pattern overview", "file_type": "document", "rationale": "The right modeling tool when an actor has a small named set of states with explicit transitions - connection lifecycles, order workflows, document approvals.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/persistence/fsm/overview.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_persistence_fsm_overview_dsl_versus_table_driven", "label": "DSL-driven FSM versus table-driven PersistentFSM", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "The two variants have different shapes: in-memory FSM is when, goto, stay plus enter and exit hooks; PersistentFSM declares a transitions table and an applyEvent reducer, and every transition is persisted and replayed.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/persistence/fsm/overview.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_persistence_fsm_overview_when_to_reach_for_fsm", "label": "When the FSM pattern fits", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "Three or more named states with distinct behaviour, explicit from-X-on-Y-go-to-Z transitions, and side effects that belong to entering a state. One state or a boolean toggle reads cleaner as an ordinary actor; hierarchical or parallel substates want a heavier library.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/persistence/fsm/overview.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_persistence_fsm_overview_fsm_versus_become", "label": "FSM versus become", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "FSM has named states, explicit goto transitions and typed state inspection, where become is anonymous closures that do not expose the current behavior; for two states become is fine, for four o